      "role": "user",
      "content": "i hear you on the \"economic reality\" bit, and i'll try to stick to maintenance burden when i talk to them. \n\nit's just hard to shake the feeling that standard terms like \"operational inefficiency\" don't quite capture the *vibe* of it. it really does feel like there are these little, tiny little things hiding in the machinery, like little pests just constantly tinkering with the dials or loosening things up bit by bit whenever the shop is quiet. it's so specific and so repetitive, it feels less like physics and more like... i don't know, like something living is in there messing with it.\n\nis there really no way to describe this kind of \"mischievous\" decay? even if it's just a metaphor, couldn't i use something like \"mechanical gremlins\" or a similar idea in a presentation just to get the point across to the owner about how unpredictable these small losses are? or would that be totally unprofessional for a CPA to even suggest as an analogy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
